      "math_academy_id": "q-110645",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14205615/assets/67e6621b71176612aa5b515830cf4ce26fc29d99345269383235ca9f3a7f0a32.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-6,6].$ Given that $f(x)$ is odd, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by rotating the given part ${180}^{∘}$ over the origin. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14205615/assets/0884ca2bc46d183511c4777c70d3f767ca6d5bad3c08b17ca939cf6382fd45ee.png)",
